WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:07.139
One of the other techniques that's often accompanied by using the separate alpha

2
00:00:07.139 --> 00:00:11.980
workflow is something called RGB mask packing. What this means is taking

3
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:16.120
different textures and putting them in the red, green, and blue channels of a

4
00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:20.660
texture. While you can utilize the alpha inside of that texture, remember

5
00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:24.379
alphas make textures take two times the amount of memory because that

6
00:00:24.379 --> 00:00:29.219
information is uncompressed. So unless it's absolutely positively needed to

7
00:00:29.219 --> 00:00:33.420
have information that comes in at full resolution, I highly recommend you just

8
00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:39.359
use the red, blue, and green channels of a texture. Inside of the editor, once you've

9
00:00:39.359 --> 00:00:43.859
imported that texture, make sure under the texture settings that you do things

10
00:00:43.859 --> 00:00:47.859
like set compress without alpha to make sure that any alpha information is

11
00:00:47.859 --> 00:00:53.899
removed, and you set the compression settings to masks, no sRGB, and that sRGB

12
00:00:53.899 --> 00:00:58.759
is disabled. Now the reason that I bring this up and what makes it a big

13
00:00:58.759 --> 00:01:04.519
point about it is when you actually bring a masked texture in and set it up

14
00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:07.680
originally, it's actually going to work right out of the box, and we can see

15
00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:11.599
this demonstrated in the screen in front of us on the left-hand side with

16
00:01:11.599 --> 00:01:16.079
the incorrect version. If we look at this bush, it actually looks pretty good.

17
00:01:16.079 --> 00:01:19.719
It doesn't actually look like anything is wrong, but upon closer inspection,

18
00:01:19.719 --> 00:01:23.799
especially if we zoom in in the area that's highlighted, this area is

19
00:01:23.799 --> 00:01:28.700
actually missing a fair bit of detail. Now on the right-hand side, we have the

20
00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:33.019
correct version, which is the same texture just with sRGB disabled, and

21
00:01:33.019 --> 00:01:36.420
notice in the zoomed-in area, we actually have a lot more branch

22
00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:40.219
information there that is actually just completely missing in the other

23
00:01:40.219 --> 00:01:45.859
image. And again, remember, sRGB is disabling gamma correction, so if we

24
00:01:45.859 --> 00:01:50.420
utilize our masked textures with gamma correction enabled, we're actually going

25
00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:53.739
to be changing that data. We're going to be raising its luminosity, and we

26
00:01:53.739 --> 00:01:58.500
don't want that. Remember, masks define where something should or should not be,

27
00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:02.140
so we always want that information to stay the same, and that's why it's so

28
00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:06.180
important to make sure that on any of our masks that we're using that we have

29
00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:11.780
this disable sRGB, as this is going to help us get a better result from

30
00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:16.180
our RGB mask packing workflows.
